{% extends 'shells/docs.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}Templates · php-via Docs{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article>
    <h1>Templates</h1>
    <p>
        A php-via page has two layers. The outer document carries the doctype, the head, the scripts that
        connect the page and your site's chrome. The view is the part that renders again on every update.
        The outer document is either a shell, a plain HTML file with placeholders, or a template that renders
        the whole document. The view is a PHP callable that returns HTML, or a template that a template
        engine renders. php-via has an adapter for Twig, which you install yourself.
    </p>

    <h2 id="two-paths">Shells and views</h2>
    <div class="code-result-split" style="align-items: start;">
        <div>
            <h3 style="margin-block-start:0">Shell: <code>shell.html</code></h3>
            <p>
                A static HTML file set with <code>withShellTemplate()</code>. php-via replaces its
                {% verbatim %}<code>{{ placeholder }}</code>{% endverbatim %} tokens with plain string
                replacement, <strong>not Twig</strong>. The shell goes out once per page load and never
                over SSE. Use it for the outer chrome: doctype, nav, footer, stylesheets.
            </p>
            {% code 'html' %}{% verbatim %}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    {{ via_head }}
    {{ head_content }}
</head>
<body>
    {{ content }}
    {{ foot_content }}
    {{ via_foot }}
</body>
</html>
            {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
        </div>
        <div>
            <h3 style="margin-block-start:0">View: callable or template</h3>
            <p>
                The page content, set with <code>$c-&gt;view()</code> and rendered again on every update. A
                callable needs nothing else; a template name needs a template engine.
            </p>
            {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
// PHP callable: no engine needed
$c->view(function () use ($count): string {
    return '<p id="count">' . $count->int() . '</p>';
});

// Twig template: signals and actions come in by name
$c->view('counter.html.twig');
            {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="engines">Template engines</h2>
    <p>
        A template view, <code>$c-&gt;view('page.html.twig')</code>, and <code>$c-&gt;render()</code> go through
        the <code>TemplateEngine</code> registered with <code>Config::withTemplateEngine()</code>. Without one,
        views are callables, and a template view, <code>render()</code> or <code>$app-&gt;getTwig()</code> throws
        a <code>LogicException</code> that names the fix.
    </p>

    <h3 id="setup">Twig</h3>
    <p>
        <code>twig/twig</code> does not come with php-via. Install it, then point php-via at the template
        directory:
    </p>
    {% code 'bash' %}{% verbatim %}
composer require twig/twig
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app = new Via(
    (new Config())
        ->withTemplateDir(__DIR__ . '/templates')    // Twig views
        ->withTwigCacheDir(__DIR__ . '/var/twig')    // optional: compiled templates on disk
        ->withShellTemplate(__DIR__ . '/shell.html') // optional: your own shell
);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        <code>withTemplateDir($dir)</code> is short for
        <code>withTemplateEngine(new TwigEngine($dir))</code>, and <code>new Via()</code> throws when Twig is
        not installed. Templates are autoescaped as HTML and strict about undefined variables. Besides
        Twig's own, they get the functions <code>bind()</code>, <code>dump()</code>, <code>via_head()</code> and
        <code>via_foot()</code>, and the global <code>basePath</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>$app-&gt;getTwig()</code> returns the <code>Twig\Environment</code>, for extensions, globals and
        templates held as strings. Register extensions right after <code>new Via()</code>, before the first
        render.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->getTwig()->addExtension(new MyExtension());
$html = $app->getTwig()->createTemplate('<p>{{ greeting }}</p>')->render(['greeting' => 'Hello']);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="own-engine">Another engine</h3>
    <p>
        Any engine works behind the <code>Rendering\TemplateEngine</code> interface: <code>render()</code> gets
        the template name, the data and, when <code>supportsBlocks()</code> says so, a block name.
        <code>via_head</code> and <code>via_foot</code> arrive in the data as <code>Rendering\Html</code>
        objects. Print them unescaped: an autoescaping engine marks <code>Html</code> as safe markup.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
use Mbolli\PhpVia\Rendering\TemplateEngine;

final class PhpTemplates implements TemplateEngine {
    public function __construct(private string $dir) {}

    public function render(string $template, array $data, ?string $block = null): string {
        extract($data);
        ob_start();
        require $this->dir . '/' . $template;

        return (string) ob_get_clean();
    }

    public function supportsBlocks(): bool {
        return false; // view(..., block:) then throws
    }
}

$config->withTemplateEngine(new PhpTemplates(__DIR__ . '/views'));
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="bootstrap">The page bootstrap: via_head and via_foot</h2>
    <p>
        Every page needs a few tags to connect to php-via: the <code>via_ctx</code> signal that names its
        context, the import map when there is one, the SSE connect with its reconnect, the beacon that closes
        the context when the tab goes, and Datastar. <code>via_head</code> writes the first four and belongs
        right after <code>&lt;meta charset&gt;</code>; <code>via_foot</code> writes the Datastar module script,
        loaded from <code>Config::getDatastarUrl()</code>, and belongs before <code>&lt;/body&gt;</code>.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>In a shell: <code>{% verbatim %}{{ via_head }}{% endverbatim %}</code> and <code>{% verbatim %}{{ via_foot }}{% endverbatim %}</code>.</li>
        <li>In a Twig layout: <code>{% verbatim %}{{ via_head() }}{% endverbatim %}</code> and <code>{% verbatim %}{{ via_foot() }}{% endverbatim %}</code>.</li>
        <li>In a callable that returns a whole document: <code>$c-&gt;viaHead()</code> and <code>$c-&gt;viaFoot()</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Every tag carries the page request's CSP nonce when middleware sets one (see
        <a href="#csp">Content-Security-Policy</a>). A page that loads its own Datastar bundle leaves
        <code>via_foot</code> out.
    </p>
    <p>
        php-via logs a warning once per shell, and once per route whose view renders a whole document, in two
        cases: the page has no <code>via_head</code>, so it never opens its stream, or it loads a Datastar
        script of its own next to an import map, which starts a second Datastar engine. Dev mode also warns
        about <code>via_head</code> without any Datastar script and about a page with more than one import map.
    </p>

    <h2 id="shell">Shells</h2>
    <p>
        Without <code>withShellTemplate()</code>, php-via uses its default shell, which loads
        <code>/via.css</code> and, in dev mode, shows a Live Signals panel. Your own shell replaces it for every
        page, and <code>$c-&gt;setShellTemplate()</code> for one page. These placeholders are filled:
    </p>
    <ul>
        {% verbatim %}
        <li><code>{{ via_head }}</code> and <code>{{ via_foot }}</code>: the bootstrap above</li>
        <li><code>{{ via_html_attrs }}</code>: the attributes of <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, <code>data-nonce</code> under a nonce (see <a href="#csp">Content-Security-Policy</a>)</li>
        <li><code>{{ content }}</code>: the rendered view</li>
        <li><code>{{ head_content }}</code> and <code>{{ foot_content }}</code>: what <code>appendToHead()</code> and <code>appendToFoot()</code> added, plus the seed of the signals' initial values</li>
        <li><code>{{ base_path }}</code>: the base path from <code>withBasePath()</code>, ending in <code>/</code></li>
        <li><code>{{ context_id }}</code>: the context's id</li>
        <li><code>{{ dev_sidebar }}</code>: the Live Signals panel in dev mode, empty otherwise</li>
        <li><code>{{ name }}</code> and <code>{{ name.id }}</code>: a signal's JSON value (HTML-escaped) and its id, where <code>name</code> is the name passed to <code>signal()</code></li>
        {% endverbatim %}
    </ul>
    <p>
        Twig tags in a shell come out as text. A worker reads each shell once; in dev mode it reads an edited
        shell again, and outside dev mode a changed shell needs a reload of the server.
    </p>

    <h2 id="twig-shell">Layouts that render the whole document</h2>
    <p>
        Instead of a shell, a Twig base template can define the whole document, and page templates extend it.
        When a view renders an <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> document, php-via skips the shell and completes the
        document instead.
    </p>
    {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
{# templates/base.html.twig #}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    {{ via_head() }}
    <title>{% block title %}{% endblock %}</title>
</head>
<body>
    <nav>...shared nav...</nav>
    <main>{% block content %}{% endblock %}</main>
    {{ via_foot() }}
</body>
</html>
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
{# templates/my-page.html.twig #}
{% extends "base.html.twig" %}

{% block title %}My App{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
    <h1>Hello</h1>
{% endblock %}
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        On the page load php-via adds the seed of the signals' initial values right after
        <code>via_head</code>'s first tag, so expressions have their values before the stream opens; a value
        the layout sets in its own <code>data-signals</code> wins. It adds what <code>appendToHead()</code> and
        <code>appendToFoot()</code> collected before the first <code>&lt;/head&gt;</code> and the last
        <code>&lt;/body&gt;</code>, on the page load and on every update, unless the exact markup is already
        there.
    </p>
    <p>
        The whole template renders again on every update, unlike a shell, which goes out once. Send only the
        part that changes with <a href="#block-updates"><code>block:</code></a>. A callable view can return a
        whole document too:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->view(fn (): string => '<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><meta charset="UTF-8">' . $c->viaHead()
    . '<title>Report</title></head><body><main id="report">...</main>' . $c->viaFoot() . '</body></html>');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        A template rendered outside a context, such as a 404 page from <code>$app-&gt;notFound()</code>
        through <code>$app-&gt;getTwig()</code>, can extend the same layout: there <code>via_head()</code> and
        <code>via_foot()</code> write only the import map and the Datastar script.
    </p>

    <h2 id="csp">Content-Security-Policy</h2>
    <p>
        A middleware that sets the <code>via.csp_nonce</code> request attribute puts its nonce on every tag
        <code>via_head</code> and <code>via_foot</code> write, the import map included, and on the Dev Bar's
        tags. Datastar needs the nonce too. It reads it from a <code>data-nonce</code> attribute on the
        <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> element, and then runs <code>data-*</code> expressions and the scripts the
        server sends, from <code>execScript()</code>, <code>dispatch()</code> or a reload, as scripts with that
        nonce. Without <code>data-nonce</code> it compiles expressions with the <code>Function</code>
        constructor, which a policy blocks without <code>'unsafe-eval'</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        The default shell writes <code>data-nonce</code> on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> when the page request
        carries a nonce. A shell of your own writes it with
        {% verbatim %}<code>{{ via_html_attrs }}</code>{% endverbatim %}, a Twig layout with
        {% verbatim %}<code>{{ via_html_attrs() }}</code>{% endverbatim %}:
    </p>
    {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en"{{ via_html_attrs() }}>
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    {{ via_head() }}
</head>
<body>
    {% block content %}{% endblock %}
    {{ via_foot() }}
</body>
</html>
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        <code>via_html_attrs</code> writes nothing without a nonce, and nothing in an update render, so a
        morph of the document does not put back the attribute Datastar removed after reading it. Dev mode warns
        once per shell or route whose page has a nonce and no <code>data-nonce</code>. The middleware sends the
        policy as a header on the response it gets back from <code>$handler-&gt;handle()</code>. This setup
        runs in Chromium with a counter, <code>dispatch()</code> and <code>execScript()</code> working and no
        CSP errors:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
final class CspNonce implements MiddlewareInterface {
    public function process(ServerRequestInterface $request, RequestHandlerInterface $handler): ResponseInterface {
        $nonce = base64_encode(random_bytes(16));
        $response = $handler->handle($request->withAttribute('via.csp_nonce', $nonce));

        return $response->withHeader('Content-Security-Policy', "default-src 'self'; script-src 'nonce-{$nonce}'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'");
    }
}

$app->middleware(new CspNonce());
$app->page('/', fn (Context $c) => $c->view('home.html.twig'));
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        A <code>&lt;meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy"&gt;</code> before <code>via_head</code> works too,
        but it cannot set <code>frame-ancestors</code>: <code>Config::withEmbeddable()</code> sends that one as a
        header, next to the middleware's. A callable that returns a whole document writes the attribute itself,
        with the nonce the page handler reads:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->page('/report', function (Context $c): void {
    $nonce = htmlspecialchars((string) $c->getRequestAttribute('via.csp_nonce'));
    $c->view(fn (): string => "<!DOCTYPE html><html data-nonce=\"{$nonce}\"><head><meta charset=\"UTF-8\">"
        . $c->viaHead() . '</head><body><main id="report">...</main>' . $c->viaFoot() . '</body></html>');
});
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="passing-data">Data in templates</h2>
    <p>
        A template gets the context's named signals as <code>Signal</code> objects and its named actions as
        <code>Action</code> objects, an action named <code>add-todo</code> as <code>addTodo</code>. It also
        gets <code>contextId</code>, <code>currentRoute</code> (the route pattern), <code>basePath</code>,
        <code>via_html_attrs</code>, <code>via_head</code>, <code>via_foot</code> and <code>_via</code>, which
        lists the names of both. What you pass in <code>$data</code> wins.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$count = $c->signal(0, 'count');
$inc   = $c->action(fn () => $count->setValue($count->int() + 1), 'inc');

// Signal 'count' → {{ count }}, a Signal: count.int, count.ref, count.id
// Action 'inc'   → {{ inc }}, an Action: inc.url
$c->view('counter.html.twig');

// Pass only what is neither a signal nor an action:
$c->view('todo.html.twig', ['todos' => $todos]);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="cache-updates">Data read on every render</h3>
    <p>
        An array passed to <code>view()</code> is built once, when the page handler runs. When the view reads
        state from elsewhere, such as a database or <code>countClients()</code>, pass a callable, which runs on
        every render:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->view('presence.html.twig', fn (): array => ['count' => $app->countClients(Scope::routeScope('/'))]);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="reactive-text">Reactive text</h2>
    <p>Use <code>data-text</code> to keep an element in step with a signal. Render the current value inside it, so the page shows it before the stream opens:</p>
    {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
<span data-text="{{ count.ref }}">{{ count.int }}</span>
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>In a PHP view, <code>$signal-&gt;ref()</code> gives the same reference:</p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->view(fn (): string => <<<HTML
    <span data-text="{$count->ref()}">{$count->int()}</span>
    HTML);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="two-way-binding">Two-way binding</h2>
    <p>Use <code>data-bind</code> for inputs that show a signal and update it:</p>
    {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
<input {{ bind(name) }} type="text">
{# or by hand: <input data-bind="{{ name.id }}" type="text"> #}
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Datastar 1.0.4 updates a bound checkbox or radio on <code>input</code>, not <code>change</code>, so a
        script that sets one has to dispatch <code>input</code>. On a web component, bind a property:
        <code>{% verbatim %}{{ bind(volume, 'value') }}{% endverbatim %}</code> renders
        <code>data-bind__prop.value</code>. See <a href="/docs/web-components#binding">Web components</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="actions">Triggering actions</h2>
    <p>Use Datastar's event attributes to post to an action's URL:</p>
    {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
<button data-on:click="@post('{{ inc.url }}')">+1</button>

{# Pass data in the query string, read with $c->input('option') #}
<button data-on:click="@post('{{ vote.url }}?option=tab')">Vote TAB</button>
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Datastar 1.0.4 cancels a request still in flight when any element sends another one with the same
        method and URL. Two elements that post the same action at the same time need a query each, such as
        <code>?from=button</code>. See <a href="/docs/actions#cancellation">Actions → Cancellation</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="template-inheritance">Template inheritance</h2>
    <p>Twig inheritance works inside views as everywhere:</p>
    {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
{# section.html.twig #}
<nav><a href="/">Home</a></nav>
<div id="section">{% block content %}{% endblock %}</div>
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
{# page.html.twig #}
{% extends "section.html.twig" %}
{% block content %}
    <h1>My page</h1>
{% endblock %}
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="block-updates">Blocks as update targets</h3>
    <p>
        Name a block, and <code>view()</code> sends only that block on updates while the page load renders the
        whole template:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->view('page.html.twig', fn (): array => ['items' => $repo->all()], block: 'demo');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        The layout, nav and static content then go out once. Give the block a single root element with an
        <code>id</code> that is unique on the page, also against the shell (the default shell uses
        <code>content</code>): Datastar morphs the element with that id. See
        <a href="/docs/views#partial-updates">Views → Partial updates</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="debugging">Debugging</h2>
    <p>The <code>dump()</code> function renders its arguments as a <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> block:</p>
    {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
{{ dump(some_variable) }}
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="/docs/views">Views</a>: updates, blocks and shared renders</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/web-components">Web components</a>: the import map and Rocket components</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/middleware">Middleware</a>: request attributes such as <code>via.csp_nonce</code></li>
        <li><a href="/docs/api#templates">API reference</a>: <code>TemplateEngine</code>, <code>Html</code> and <code>TwigEngine</code></li>
    </ul>
</article>
{% endblock %}
